40c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1031 "The Sum and Difference Formulas for Sine" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1033 270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1034 1.4866534924833008 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049773 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "124fce70-48dc-5897-8dfb-8d65e4d68415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1031 "Using the Pythagorean Identity in the First Quadrant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1033 1453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1034 0.7993983415385711 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050714 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "60b97f00-f86a-5a4f-834e-1a83f8ee3cc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184052 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184052 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u =  -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184052 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c049aaef-f59a-5113-b739-20cc7a6d802c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184052 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u},$ $\\cos{u},$ and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{v},$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3,$ and $a+b = 15.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184132 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184132 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06df772e-eb43-55e8-9ede-3fc2f7ae291d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184132 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right)\n \t\t\t\t -  \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left( \\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {72} {125} -  \\dfrac {28} {125} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {100} {125}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {4} {5}.\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184142 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184226 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184422 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184422 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184422 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184422 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184422 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Trigonometric Ratios Using the Sum Formula for Sine" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184422 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186185017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184422 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184422 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9f058771-3446-5ded-a35e-dcaf0a14a90a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184892 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184892 #edb/ref 1061 11735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1da36f0e-cde3-5812-8b1a-eeab11974263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185017 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185017 #edb/ref 1061 37604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185017 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28ed07fc-351f-5531-9103-a17479e1cce6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185017 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185018 #edb/ref 1061 37605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185018 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c891011-e58f-5ba0-8625-0854ca6c6fa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185018 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185347 #edb/ref 1064 "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for sine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v = \\dfrac{3}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\sin u = -\\dfrac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\sin\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant? \n\nFirst, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{v}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{u}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}u & =1-{sin}^{2}u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{12}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{4}{5})-(-\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}+\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185347 #edb/ref 1065 6203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185347 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "699a9021-a261-5b19-b767-75fd52b030bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185347 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250857 #edb/ref 1080 "21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250857 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250857 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "236be099-00d2-5a93-807c-c0611363dd3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251816 #edb/ref 1080 "13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251816 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251816 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "936b5a6a-37a9-599d-94c5-7ae7a2280c0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252163 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252163 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252163 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b6bc72ad-8c01-5974-9602-15da4003854b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252340 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252340 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252340 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c9b770b1-d1d3-52d1-84af-ef96a46675ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252366 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cd8f375c-b6b5-5356-9e90-212803b05a94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186250857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186251816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186252921 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "42b3f8d2-ad82-503b-8cd5-80330391b6f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253580 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{55}}{42},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1267137-e6ff-5440-b525-7d34538d465f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253580 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{55}}{42}+\\frac{10}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-3\\sqrt{55}}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=10,b=3,$ and $a+b=13.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
